{"text": " Winning a client once is a transaction. Keeping a client for two years is a business. The difference between a freelancer earning 80,000 Kenyan shillings per month from eight different clients and 200,000 Kenyan shillings per month from three repeat clients is relationship management. This lesson builds the system. In this lesson, Western clients return to the same contractor when two conditions are met. One, the work was excellent and two, they remember you exist when they have a new need. Both conditions require active management. Excellent work does not guarantee being remembered and being remembered does not compensate for mediocre work. The relationship management system has four components. Post project follow-up, 30 days after delivery, check whether the system is working as expected and offer to answer any questions. This produces warm referrals 30% of the time. Quarterly touch points, every 90 days, send a brief personal email, saw that something relevant to their business or industry happened, thought of you. How is the project you built working out? New capability updates. When you complete a new course or build something new that is relevant to their business, send a two sentence update. I recently built new capability. This might be useful if you ever want to expand what the system does. And retainer conversations. At six months post project, offer a maintenance and evolution retainer. Most systems need updates, new documents or feature additions. And a retainer gives the client certainty of access to your time. The most powerful retention tool is results documentation. At three months post delivery, ask the client for usage metrics, how many queries per day, what staff time it has saved, any notable outcomes. Compile these into a brief result summary and send it to them. Two things happen. One, they re-experience the value they received. Two, they realise you care about outcomes, not just delivery. Contractors who follow up on results are rare and are remembered when the next project emerges. Apply what you learn in this lesson and you will see results immediately. Your skills bear fruit.", "segments": [{"id": 0, "seek": 0, "start": 0.0, "end": 2.94, "text": " Winning a client once is a transaction.", "tokens": [50364, 10427, 773, 257, 6423, 1564, 307, 257, 14425, 13, 50552], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1717100264150885, "compression_ratio": 1.726775956284153, "no_speech_prob": 0.013370916247367859, "words": [{"word": " Winning", "start": 0.0, "end": 0.44, "probability": 0.8079128861427307}, {"word": " a", "start": 0.44, "end": 0.58, "probability": 0.9942334294319153}, {"word": " client", "start": 0.58, "end": 0.94, "probability": 0.9962626099586487}, {"word": " once", "start": 0.94, "end": 1.38, "probability": 0.7855991125106812}, {"word": " is", "start": 1.38, "end": 2.04, "probability": 0.9882577061653137}, {"word": " a", "start": 2.04, "end": 2.2, "probability": 0.9980019927024841}, {"word": " transaction.", "start": 2.2, "end": 2.94, "probability": 0.9879051446914673}]}, {"id": 1, "seek": 0, "start": 3.92, "end": 7.14, "text": " Keeping a client for two years is a business.", "tokens": [50552, 30187, 257, 6423, 337, 732, 924, 307, 257, 1606, 13, 50784], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1717100264150885, "compression_ratio": 1.726775956284153, "no_speech_prob": 0.013370916247367859, "words": [{"word": " Keeping", "start": 3.92, "end": 4.14, "probability": 0.961961030960083}, {"word": " a", "start": 4.14, "end": 4.42, "probability": 0.9951893091201782}, {"word": " client", "start": 4.42, "end": 4.78, "probability": 0.9987439513206482}, {"word": " for", "start": 4.78, "end": 5.0, "probability": 0.9981004595756531}, {"word": " two", "start": 5.0, "end": 5.3, "probability": 0.9020248055458069}, {"word": " years", "start": 5.3, "end": 5.78, "probability": 0.9987990856170654}, {"word": " is", "start": 5.78, "end": 6.44, "probability": 0.9843651056289673}, {"word": " a", "start": 6.44, "end